# Response headers for https://headwater.tools/, which serves two halves out of
# one directory: the hand-built pages committed under `site/`, and the pages
# `mkdocs build` renders from this corpus. `tools/assemble-site.sh` composes
# them, and HW-DR-0047 rules how they share the host.
#
# WHY THE POLICY IS WRITTEN LOOSE FIRST AND STRICT SECOND
#
#   The two halves need different content policies. A hand-built page inlines
#   everything it uses and reaches nothing at read time, which is the
#   measurement HW-DR-0037 states. A generated page links its stylesheet, its
#   script, its web fonts and its search index as files beside it, so a policy
#   of `default-src 'none'` serves it with no stylesheet, no script and no
#   search.
#
#   The generated half is open-ended — a new shelf adds a new top-level path —
#   and the hand-built half is a closed set that HW-DR-0037 names. So the
#   wider policy is the default under `/*`, and every hand-built path below
#   re-asserts the narrow one.
#
#   That order is deliberate and it is correct under either reading of what
#   this file does with a header two rules both set. If the later rule wins,
#   a hand-built path takes the narrow policy. If both headers are sent and
#   the browser enforces their intersection, a hand-built path takes the
#   narrow policy too, because it is a subset of the wide one. Writing it the
#   other way round is correct under only one of the two.
#
# WHAT THE WIDE POLICY ADMITS, AND WHY EACH TERM IS THERE
#
#   `'unsafe-inline'` for script is not a preference. The theme emits two
#   inline blocks on every generated page, and one of them declares the
#   `base_url` and `shortcuts` values that `js/base.js` reads to run search.
#   Refusing inline script refuses search, which is a capability #527 requires
#   the served page to have. The inline content is this repository's own build
#   output on its own origin.
#
#   `https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com` is where the built-in MkDocs theme loads
#   highlight.js and its two stylesheets, on 277 of the generated pages. It is
#   third-party code, it carries no subresource integrity, and this corpus's
#   own subject is provenance — so it is recorded as a debt in
#   HW-OBL-0160 rather than accepted quietly. Removing the CDN is not a
#   one-line change: `js/darkmode.js` dereferences the two `hljs-*` link
#   elements with no null guard, so deleting them throws on page load, where
#   refusing them at the network keeps the elements and costs only the
#   coloring. Vendoring the three files is the remedy that record names.
#
#   `worker-src` and `connect-src` are what search needs: `search/main.js`
#   starts a worker, and the worker reads `search/search_index.json`.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The hand-built half, which HW-DR-0037 governs by name. Each path re-asserts
# the policy that half has always carried, unchanged from what it served
# before the generated half shared the host.
/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/how-it-works/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/compare/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/proof/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/tutorial/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/glossary/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/changelog/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/ns/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

# The corpus descriptor is what every generated page names in its
# `rel="describedby"` link, and spec 7 expects a cold reader to fetch it from
# another origin. `/ns/*` above carries the same header for the same reason.
/corpus.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
